>> title: Manakish
>> category: Flatbreads & Pancakes
>> tags: bread, yeast, zaatar, lebanese, oven, breakfast
>> counters: Shawarma Counter, Bakery
>> aka: manakish, manaeesh, manakeesh, manoushe, man'oushe, manoushi, manousheh, zaatar bread, za'atar flatbread, Lebanese breakfast bread
>> pairs-with: zaatar, labneh, pita-bread
>> servings: 6
>> time: 2 hr 30 min
>> slack: forgiving — an hour and a half rising or two is the same dough and it can be shaped straight from the fridge; the za'atar is the part with a window, because it is oil and dried herb over a hot oven and it goes from fragrant to acrid without changing colour much
>> step.1: proof the yeast 10 min
>> step.2: knead 8 min to a soft dough
>> step.3: prove 1 1/2 hr until doubled
>> step.4: slacken the za'atar with oil
>> step.5: roll into rounds, dimple all over
>> step.6: bake at 450°F 8 min, pale and soft

Whisk @instant yeast{2%tsp}(6 g) and @granulated sugar{1%tsp}(4 g) into @warm water{1 1/4%cups}(300 mL) and ~stand{10%min} until it froths.

Knead @&(~1)yeast water{} with @all-purpose flour{3 1/2%cups}(455 g), @fine sea salt{2%tsp}(12 g), @olive oil{3%Tbs}(45 mL) and @whole milk{2%Tbs}(30 mL) ~knead{8%min}, until the dough is soft, slightly slack and springs back slowly. Softer than a pita dough: manakish is not meant to balloon and split, it is meant to fold in half around itself and stay bendy.

Prove @&(~1)dough{} covered in a warm place ~prove{1 1/2%hr}, until doubled.

Stir @za'atar{1/2%cup}(60 g) into @extra-virgin olive oil{1/3%cup}(80 mL) until it is a loose, spoonable slurry, and let it ~stand{15%min}. Za'atar spooned on dry scorches in the oven and tastes of dust; the oil is what carries it and what stops it burning.

Divide @&(~2)proved dough{} into six, roll each into an eight-inch round about a quarter-inch thick, and dimple all over with your fingertips so the topping has somewhere to sit rather than sliding to the edge.

Bake @&(~1)dimpled rounds{} spread with @&(~2)za'atar oil{} on a hot #baking steel{} in the #oven{} at 450°F (230°C) ~bake{8%min}, until the bases are set and freckled but the bread is still pale and soft. This is not pizza — brown it and you have made a cracker you cannot fold, which is how it is eaten, in the hand, on the way somewhere.
